<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Melfi</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Melfi is a town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basilicata">Basilicata</a>, southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, on a hill near Monte Vulture. Around 1042 the Normans of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apulia">Apulia</a> made it their first capital, and in 1059 Pope <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicholas-ii">Nicholas II</a> held a synod there at which he invested <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-guiscard">Robert Guiscard</a> as duke of Apulia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calabria">Calabria</a>. In 1231 Emperor Frederick II issued there the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitutions-of-melfi">Constitutions of Melfi</a>, a law code for the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/melfi.jpg" alt="Melfi" width="320" height="425" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Stratokumulus · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Melfi_-_Municipio_di_Melfi_-_2025-09-08.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The popes were weak military rulers and usually relied on others to protect them: the Franks against the Lombards, the Normans of southern Italy, who became papal vassals at <b>Melfi</b> in 1059, and later France, Spain or Austria.</blockquote><a href="/empire/papal-states">Papal States · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Pope Urban II opened a synod at <b>Melfi</b>, attended by seventy bishops and twelve abbots, on church reform and relations with the Greek Church.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/september-10">10 September in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Norman mercenaries in Byzantine Apulia elected William Iron Arm as their count at <b>Melfi</b> around 1042–1043.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-apulia-and-calabria">Duchy of Apulia and Calabria</a></li></ul></section>
